# ============================================================
# Line-ending normalization
# ============================================================
# Everything is stored in the repo as LF, regardless of which OS the file
# was committed from. Without this, files edited on Windows get committed
# with CRLF, and two concrete things break:
#
#   1. .env parsing - a trailing \r ends up inside the value, so DB_HOST
#      becomes "postgres\r" and the connection fails with a confusing error
#      that looks nothing like a line-ending problem.
#   2. Shell scripts and SQL files copied into a Linux Docker image fail to
#      run, since the interpreter sees the \r as part of the command.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` means: Git decides which files are text, and always
# checks them out with LF - including on Windows. Both VS Code and PyCharm
# handle LF files natively, so nothing needs changing in either editor.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicitly binary - never normalize these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary